WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.982
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:32.860
 

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:35.970
HA: People are surprised that MIT has a public art

00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:40.600
program and it's been going on since 1965, really.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:43.260
We think of the entire
campus as a museum

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:46.810
and we have over 66
large-scale permanent art

00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:48.930
installed throughout campus.

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:52.910
UPITIS: We are known to be a technology school.

00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:55.680
However, ever since World War II,

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:59.990
MIT has really thought of the arts as important, both

00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:04.857
in terms of curricular but
also in the life on campus

00:01:04.857 --> 00:01:07.000
and the life of the students.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:08.510
 

00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:12.900
Alexander Calder's The Big Sail from 1965

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:17.130
is MIT's first major
commission of public art.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:20.650
Calder's Big Sail
really has this function

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:25.780
of humanizing the
post-war MIT campus.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:30.520
This is a term that MIT
administration used in support

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:32.600
of why there were arts at MIT

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:37.310
this idea of humanizing
scientists and technologists.

00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:40.250
President Wiesner was
an enormous supporter

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:42.960
of art at MIT.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:48.750
And he was integral
in MIT's adoption

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:51.590
of a Percent for
Art policy, which

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:54.550
allocates a portion of
every building project

00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:58.220
or major renovation budget
to new art in a public space

00:01:58.220 --> 00:01:59.800
on the campus.

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:06.010
And this policy was passed
in 1968 by President Johnson.

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:11.210
MIT's Percent for Art is very
rare for a private university.

00:02:11.210 --> 00:02:15.720
It is the oldest in the country
and one of the very few.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:19.260
HA: In 1975, the IM
Pei Chemistry Building

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:21.320
was the first Percent
for Art program.

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:24.060
And that was a Louise Nevelson.

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:28.420
UPITIS: IM suggested
Nevelson as the artist

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:34.190
to create a work for the
newly-built Building 66.

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:37.710
And it is her second
public art commission.

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:40.580
She had just started
working at that scale.

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:43.690
When the work was
installed, it was actually

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:46.800
placed in the students'
volleyball court.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:49.710
And students had no
idea this was happening.

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:54.372
And students became quite
irritated and a series of hacks

00:02:54.372 --> 00:02:55.760
ensued.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:57.890
HA: We learned a valuable
lesson with that.

00:02:57.890 --> 00:03:02.240
And so we do involve
students now in our process.

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:05.870
What I find so exciting and
fantastic about MIT is in a way,

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:07.950
we collect art and architecture.

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:10.330
As you walk around,
you are surrounded

00:03:10.330 --> 00:03:13.610
by creativity and the
physical presence of art.

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:16.500
Buildings like the
Frank Gehry, the Stata,

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:19.300
which is an artificial
intelligence and computer

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:20.670
science building.

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:23.690
Or the Stephen Hall Building,
which is a residence.

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:28.400
It really is just an amazing
gift to the visitors.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:32.920
UPITIS: Dan Graham's Yin
Yang Pavillion is from 2003.

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:37.700
It takes the form of
a very large yin yang.

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:41.440
Students have really engaged
with the piece in a very MIT

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:44.880
way, they placed
small rubber duck's

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:47.720
in the water portion
of the work, which

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:51.250
can be a challenge in terms
of maintaining the work.

00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:53.720
But the artist
actually really enjoys

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:57.260
this gesture of interaction
on the part of the students.

00:03:57.260 --> 00:04:00.000
HA: For those who know
about the MIT Percent

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.060
for Art Program and the
public art collection,

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:04.522
it is considered world class.

00:04:04.522 --> 00:04:06.230
But one of the messages
I want to get out

00:04:06.230 --> 00:04:09.550
is that most Bostonians don't
know that this public art

00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:10.320
exists here.

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:11.810
And it's free.

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:14.360
Always open to the public.

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:17.380
And so we want everyone to
know about the collection.

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:20.430
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:33.110
 

